rules.rule:
What: Use TanStack Query hooks with proper query keys structure for data fetching

Why: Ensures consistent caching, invalidation, and data synchronization across the app

Good:
export const paymentRequestKeys = {
  all: ['paymentRequests'] as const,
  byProperty: (propertyId: string) => [...paymentRequestKeys.all, 'property', propertyId] as const,
};

export const usePaymentRequestsByProperty = (propertyId: string) => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: paymentRequestKeys.byProperty(propertyId),
    queryFn: () => getPaymentRequestsByProperty(propertyId),
  });
};

Bad:
export const usePaymentRequests = (propertyId: string) => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['payments', propertyId], // No centralized key structure
    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/payments?property=${propertyId}`),
  });
};

What: Use console.error with context instead of console.log for error scenarios

Why: Makes debugging easier by providing context and proper error severity levels

Good:
console.error('Payment request approval failed:', error);

Bad:
console.log(error);

What: Use the centralized apiClient from lib/api/client.ts instead of direct fetch calls

Why: Ensures consistent auth headers, error handling, and request formatting across all API calls

Good:
import { apiClient } from './client';
export async function getRentHistory(id: string) {
  return apiClient.get<RentHistoryResponse>(`/rent-history/${id}`);
}

Bad:
export async function getRentHistory(id: string) {
  const token = localStorage.getItem('authToken');
  const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/rent-history/${id}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }
  });
  return response.json();
}

What: Export all TypeScript interfaces and types used in API responses

Why: Enables type reuse across components and ensures type safety throughout the application

Good:
export interface RentHistoryItem {
  _id: string;
  tenant: TenantInfo;
  room: RoomInfo;
  totalAmount: number;
}

Bad:
interface RentHistoryItem { // Not exported
  _id: string;
  tenant: any;
  room: any;
}

What: Wrap page components with AuthGuard and specify allowedRoles for role-based access control

Why: Prevents unauthorized access and ensures users only see pages appropriate for their role

Good:
export default function AdminDashboard() {
  return (
    <AuthGuard allowedRoles={['admin']}>
      <AdminDashboardContent />
    </AuthGuard>
  );
}

Bad:
export default function AdminDashboard() {
  const { user } = useAuth();
  if (user?.role !== 'admin') return null;
  return <AdminDashboardContent />;
}

What: Use FormData for API requests that include file uploads instead of JSON

Why: Proper file upload handling and multipart/form-data content type

Good:
const formData = new FormData();
formData.append('amount', data.amount.toString());
if (data.paymentProof) {
  formData.append('paymentProof', data.paymentProof);
}
return apiClient.post('/payments', formData);

Bad:
return apiClient.post('/payments', {
  amount: data.amount,
  paymentProof: data.paymentProof // File object in JSON
});

What: Always invalidate related queries in mutation onSuccess handlers to keep data fresh

Why: Ensures UI reflects the latest data after mutations without manual refetching

Good:
export const useApprovePaymentRequest = () => {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: approvePaymentRequest,
    onSuccess: (data) => {
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({
        queryKey: paymentRequestKeys.byProperty(data.property._id),
      });
    },
  });
};

Bad:
export const useApprovePaymentRequest = () => {
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: approvePaymentRequest,
    // No cache invalidation
  });
};

What: Add 'use client' directive at the top of components that use React hooks or browser APIs

Why: Next.js App Router requires explicit client component marking for client-side features

Good:
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
export function MyComponent() {
  const [state, setState] = useState();
  return <div>...</div>;
}

Bad:
import { useState } from 'react';
export function MyComponent() {
  const [state, setState] = useState();
  return <div>...</div>;
}

What: Define and use consistent response wrapper types for all API endpoints

Why: Standardizes error handling and response parsing across the application

Good:
export interface ApiResponse<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data: T;
  message?: string;
}

export async function getProperties(): Promise<ApiResponse<Property[]>> {
  return apiClient.get<ApiResponse<Property[]>>('/properties');
}

Bad:
export async function getProperties(): Promise<Property[]> {
  const response = await apiClient.get('/properties');
  return response.data; // Assuming structure
}

What: Use NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix for client-side environment variables and access them through config.ts

Why: Ensures proper environment variable exposure and centralized configuration management

Good:
// lib/config.ts
export const config = {
  api: {
    baseUrl: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL || 'http://localhost:5000/api/v1',
  },
};

// Usage
import { config } from '@/lib/config';
const apiUrl = config.api.baseUrl;

Bad:
const apiUrl = process.env.API_URL; // Not exposed to client
const apiUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL; // Direct access, not centralized
